data.content:
Yes — these are **known MSTest v4 behavioral (runtime) changes**, not compilation issues. You have two problems:

---

## 1. `TestContext.TestName` crashes in `[ClassInitialize]`

MSTest v4 now **throws at runtime** when you access `TestContext.TestName` in `[ClassInitialize]` — it's only valid inside `[TestInitialize]` or test methods (per-test context doesn't exist yet at class init time).

**Fix:** Remove the `TestName` access from `ClassSetup`, or move it to `[TestInitialize]`:

```csharp
[ClassInitialize]
public static void ClassSetup(TestContext context)
{
    // TestContext.TestName is not available here in v4
    System.Console.WriteLine("Class setup complete");
}

[TestInitialize]
public void TestSetup()
{
    System.Console.WriteLine($"Setting up for: {TestContext.TestName}");
}
```

## 2. Discovery errors

MSTest v4 sets **`TreatDiscoveryWarningsAsErrors`** to `true` by default. Any discovery warning (e.g., the crash in `ClassInitialize`) now fails the entire discovery phase rather than being silently ignored.

Once you fix the `TestName` crash above, discovery should succeed. If you want a temporary workaround while investigating, create a `.runsettings` file:

```xml
<RunSettings>
  <MSTest>
    <TreatDiscoveryWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatDiscoveryWarningsAsErrors>
  </MSTest>
</RunSettings>
```

